          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is anemia, and does it always mean low iron?",
          "answer": "**Anemia** means a lower than normal amount of hemoglobin, the protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen around the body. It is a finding on a blood test, not a disease in itself.\n\n**No, anemia does not always mean low iron.** Iron deficiency is the most common cause worldwide, but it is not the only one. Vitamin B12 and folate deficiencies are also recognized causes of anemia. The guidance I found does not go into detail on chronic disease, bone marrow problems, or kidney problems, so I can't describe those from these sources.\n\nThe guidance also says that in adults, iron deficiency is a finding that needs explaining. Iron is mostly lost through bleeding, so the question is where the iron went. A clinician can work out the cause with blood tests and further questions. Treating the low count without finding the cause can miss a serious problem.\n\n*Source: Iron-deficiency anemia (general clinical reference); ICD-10-CM Chapter 3 (blood disorders).*\n\nIf you have vomiting blood, black or tarry stools, or bleeding with dizziness, fainting, or shortness of breath, seek emergency care right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
